Let an array parameter's bound name an earlier parameter, as C99 says:
void f(int n, int a[n]), void g(int n, int m, int a[n][m]) with a[i][j]
and sizeof a[0], and the prototype void h(int n, int a[*]). Upstream's
mob branch made the same change in three commits that do not apply to
0.9.27, whose parser they postdate: ec5d9429 of 2022-03-01 ("tccgen:
accept array-size expressions in function paramters") makes the
parameters visible to the bounds, 313855c2 of 2022-03-21 ("Fix multi
dimensional vla arrays on stack") keeps a bound's tokens and evaluates
them on entry to the function, and 0244320b of 2022-03-24 with a5588501
of 2022-04-12 lets the outermost bound be empty, int a[][m]. This patch
follows their design.

TinyCC 0.9.27 pushes a parameter as a field symbol, which no lookup finds,
so a bound that named one failed with "'n' undeclared"; and at file scope a
bound had to be a constant expression. post_type now makes each named
parameter visible, at a scope of its own, to the bounds of the parameters
after it until its list ends (C99 6.2.1p4 and p7). The declaration list of
an old-style definition, void f(n, a) int n; int a[n]; {}, is such a list
too, in the order of its declarations. A bound inside a parameter list is
read into a token string and typed with no code, since the parameter it
names has no storage yet. An integer constant expression gives an array as
before. Any other bound gives a VLA whose element symbol keeps the tokens:
one that names a parameter, and one with a store, a call or a read of a
volatile object, int a[(n++, 3)] or int a[(v + 1, 3)], which folds to a
constant but whose side effects the function must have. Such an effect
counts only where the typing would generate code for it, at nocode_wanted
1, so that sizeof (n++) and a skipped operand leave a constant. A read is
counted where gv loads a volatile lvalue and where gen_cast takes one,
since gen_op's cast drops the qualifier before the load. The element
symbol owns the kept tokens, and sym_free frees them with it; the replay's
begin_macro(..., 2) leaves them to their owner. A constant bound inside a
VLA, the 3 of int a[][3][n], keeps its value. The parameter's type is adjusted to a pointer to the element, which
for int a[n][m] is a VLA row, and convert_parameter_type now clears VT_VLA
as it clears VT_ARRAY. On entry to the function, after gfunc_prolog has
made the parameters locals, func_vla_params evaluates the saved bounds
(C99 6.9.1p10): the outermost bound of an array parameter for its side
effects only, and the bound of each VLA the parameter points to, innermost
first, into a size_t slot that an index and sizeof read. Each bound is
read with the bindings it had where it was written: a parameter not yet
visible there is hidden, so that n in int n = 3; int f(int a[][n], int n)
is the global one. GCC reads the bounds of an old-style definition only
where a size is first used, and the side effects of an outermost one
never; this patch evaluates them on entry, as C99 says and as GCC does for
a prototype. The qualifiers and static of a parameter's array are accepted,
and [*] gives a VLA of unspecified size, marked as such, which a function
definition refuses at any depth, as GCC does.

compare_types took an array of variable length and an array of fixed size
for different types, so passing int arr[2][3] to int a[n][m] warned of an
incompatible pointer, and a definition after a prototype with a fixed
bound was refused; by C99 6.7.5.2p6 they are compatible.

A function with no parameter of variably modified type compiles to the
same code as before: no bound is evaluated and no slot is allocated.

